      "ballot_id": "B051",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Kenneth J. Schafer",
        "Maciej Lewenstein",
        "Paul B. Corkum"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The recollision picture gave a remarkably simple physical account of high-harmonic generation and opened a route to observing electron motion on its natural timescale. It is a foundational, experimentally fruitful contribution to attosecond science.",
        "attribution": "The proposed trio represents complementary theory and physical insight. The model developed across theory and experiment, so the precise division of conceptual credit deserves care.",
        "maturity": "The framework has been tested and extended across many strong-field and attosecond experiments over several decades.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery is collective and the boundaries between the foundational model, its theoretical formulation, and later experimental realization are not sharp."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B008",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Hidetoshi Katori",
        "Jun Ye"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks have transformed precision timekeeping and enabled tests of fundamental physics with unprecedented sensitivity. Their precision also makes them a powerful tool for sensing and comparing gravitational potential.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye led independent, complementary advances that established the approach and pushed it to exceptional performance.",
        "maturity": "The method is mature, replicated across laboratories, and has sustained improvements in accuracy and stability.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field's progress is distributed across many groups, so a prize would need to define whether it recognizes the clock concept, its realization, or the precision milestone."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B025",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Christian J. Bordé",
        "David E. Pritchard",
        "Mark A. Kasevich"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Atom interferometry brings wave interference to precise measurements of acceleration, rotation, and fundamental quantities. It is a powerful and conceptually clean extension of quantum mechanics into precision sensing.",
        "attribution": "The three names represent important foundational and experimental strands, but the technique was developed by a broader community.",
        "maturity": "The method is well established, with repeated demonstrations and increasingly precise applications in fundamental tests and sensing.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The nominated contribution spans several related interferometer implementations, making the central prize-worthy advance and its credit boundary somewhat diffuse."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B017",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Peter F. Moulton",
        "Ursula Keller",
        "Wilson Sibbett"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The titanium-sapphire laser and passive mode locking made robust, broadly tunable femtosecond pulses practical and helped enable ultrafast science. This is a major instrument advance with deep consequences for atomic physics.",
        "attribution": "The listed scientists represent important contributions to the laser medium and distinct mode-locking methods. The bundled discovery contains several advances with different histories.",
        "maturity": "The technologies have been widely used and refined for decades, with clear influence on ultrafast and attosecond experiments.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "It is difficult to treat the laser medium, Kerr-lens mode locking, and semiconductor saturable absorbers as one indivisible discovery or to apportion credit among their developers."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B009",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Gerald Gabrielse",
        "Jeffrey S. Hangst"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Trapping and precisely measuring antimatter enables stringent tests of matter–antimatter symmetry and fundamental interactions. The work has made a uniquely controlled laboratory subject out of a rare form of matter.",
        "attribution": "Gabrielse and Hangst represent major, sustained contributions to precision trapping and antimatter experiments.",
        "maturity": "Antihydrogen production and spectroscopy have advanced substantially, and the experimental program has produced increasingly precise results.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The most decisive comparisons with ordinary matter remain limited by experimental precision, so the fundamental tests are still developing."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B006",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Ataç İmamoğlu",
        "Peter Michler",
        "Yoshihisa Yamamoto"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum-dot sources established semiconductor artificial atoms as practical emitters of single and indistinguishable photons, an important bridge between solid-state physics and quantum optics.",
        "attribution": "The three candidates represent influential demonstrations and development across distinct research groups; the advance rests on broader work in materials, cavity physics, and photon characterization.",
        "maturity": "The sources are well studied and repeatedly demonstrated, though performance and integration continue to improve.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "It is unclear whether the central recognition should be for the artificial-atom concept, indistinguishable-photon demonstrations, or subsequent device performance."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B005",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Heino Falcke",
        "Sheperd S. Doeleman"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Horizon-scale imaging made strong-gravity structure around a black hole directly accessible and provided a striking test of relativistic predictions. It is an exceptional observational achievement.",
        "attribution": "Falcke's theoretical and observational leadership and Doeleman's role in developing the observing effort are important, but the result depended on a large international collaboration and many instrumental contributions.",
        "maturity": "The result has been followed by additional imaging and analysis, establishing the technique and its scientific value.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Individual credit is difficult to separate from the collaboration's collective work, and an image of the emission around a black hole is not a direct image of the event horizon itself."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B003",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Chandrashekhar Joshi",
        "Toshiki Tajima",
        "Wim Leemans"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Plasma wakefield acceleration offers a striking route to very large accelerating gradients and has produced important experimental demonstrations. Its potential could substantially change accelerator physics.",
        "attribution": "Tajima and Dawson's early proposal, Joshi's experimental development, and Leemans's major demonstrations represent distinct stages; the listed names do not capture the full breadth of the field.",
        "maturity": "The physical principle is well established, but practical, reliable acceleration with beam quality and control suitable for broad use remains under development.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The long-term scientific impact is not yet clear because the technology has not reached the performance and reproducibility of conventional accelerator systems."
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
